Home-made trial image (2026-11-19) - NOT a vendor build.
  mcb_0923_pinks_no13.bin
Made here with tools/fw_waveform.py from the production image FW_260923
(fw2029.09.23/MCB_16_0923.bin) and the vendor's 11/9 candidates:
  colour 0x0A <- pink red No.13    (FW_261109/mcb_pink_red_0_16.bin, colour 12)
  colour 0x0C <- pink purple No.13 (FW_261109/mcb_pink_purple_0_7.bin, colour 12)
These are the 11/9 "No.13" pinks, NOT the 11/18 ones: for FW_261118 the
vendor retuned the two pinks AND changed the other colours' timing (the
whole table went from 662 to 1197).
Only those two entries of the colour waveform table differ from FW_260923
(step 0 padded to FW_260923's total of 662, one level-0 hold of 4 split in
two to keep the byte count); size and every other byte are FW_260923's.
The 4 table-header bytes at image offset 0xCF7A..0xCF7D (c9 99 cc 5c) are
left as in FW_260923. Static reading of the firmware shows they are a
transfer mask: the MCU sends them to the display controller with command
0xA4 and then XORs every 32-byte waveform transfer (command 0x10) with the
same 4 bytes; nothing is read back after 0xA4, and no rule ties their value
to the table's contents (CRC/sum searches over every vendor table found
none). Keeping the base's value keeps every controller register payload
identical to FW_260923. Not proven on hardware.
Unproven on hardware: try it on a spare board first.
Reproduce (from the repo root; it refuses while the .bin exists - delete
it first or add --force):
  python tools/fw_waveform.py transplant --base FW/FW_260923/fw2029.09.23/MCB_16_0923.bin --set 10=FW/archive/FW_261109/mcb_pink_red_0_16.bin:12 --set 12=FW/archive/FW_261109/mcb_pink_purple_0_7.bin:12 --out FW/archive/FW_261118B/mcb_0923_pinks_no13.bin
